--TEST--
Core\Arr::slice renumbers every key its preserveKeys option discards, where array_slice renumbers the integers and keeps the strings
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
`rule:types/preserve-keys` removes every rule that asks what type a key is, and array_slice's `$preserve_keys = false` is one: it renumbers the integer keys of the window and keeps the string ones, so the same call answers a list or a map depending on what the subject's keys happened to be. Novis's `{preserveKeys: false}` discards every key alike and the result is a list from "0", whatever went in; `{preserveKeys: true}` keeps every key alike, and that half of the option does agree with PHP entry for entry.
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Render {
    public static function of(array<int> $a): string {
        var $out = "";
        foreach ($a as string $k => int $v) {
            $out = $out . $k . "=" . $v . ";";
        }
        return $out;
    }
}

// No integer key at all: `array_slice($map, 1, 2)` answers `y=2;z=3;`, keeping
// the two strings, where Novis numbers the window from zero.
array<int> $map = ["x" => 1, "y" => 2, "z" => 3, "w" => 4];
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::slice($map, 1, 2)), "\n";

// Both kinds of key in one subject, which is where PHP's rule is at its least
// predictable: `array_slice($mixed, 0, 3)` answers `0=1;x=2;1=3;`, renumbering
// the two integers around a string it left alone — three entries whose keys
// now depend on how many *earlier* entries were integer-keyed. Novis's window is
// a list.
array<int> $mixed = ["5" => 1, "x" => 2, "9" => 3, "b" => 4];
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::slice($mixed, 0, 3)), "\n";

// The agreeing half, on the same subject: asked to keep the keys, both
// languages keep all of them, so the divergence is entirely in what `false`
// means and not in which entries the window names.
echo Render::of(Core\Arr::slice($mixed, 0, 3, {preserveKeys: true})), "\n";
--EXPECT--
0=2;1=3;
0=1;1=2;2=3;
5=1;x=2;9=3;
